## Verdict

**The implementation is essentially complete against intent.** Every substantive
commitment of the authored spec shipped, all §7.2 / §8 acceptance numbers matched
on the first attempt, and the deliberate "reproduce first, tweak never" narrowing
was executed exactly as the operator asked in `003-comments.txt` — with the six
excluded candidates documented in the test's top-of-file XML block as required.
The four spec→impl deviations of mechanism (#13, #15, #16, #17) are each a case of
a worker declining to follow an instruction that was wrong or impossible, with
evidence, and each was upheld by the code judge. In every case the spec is what
needs correcting, not the code.

**The highest-priority gap is #19 — the unguarded handedness sign**, and it is
the only one with real latent risk. The chain is: the preliminary's sign
convention (#1) and its CPL-CPL consistency check (#2) both failed to enter the
spec; the implementation nonetheless got the sign right by reading the reference;
but the only tests that would detect a regression skip themselves on any machine
lacking the sibling `optics-mueller` checkout. A future refactor could flip
circular handedness and see a green suite. This is exactly the class of silent
failure the spec's Part A worried about for `vec` ordering — and there, the answer
was a data-free transpose-guard unit test. The same treatment is warranted here: a
pure, always-run fact pinning the source-`+`/analyzer-`−` convention, or the
CPL-CPL check the reference already provides.

Second priority is the pair of **spec-text corrections** (#13's §0
`[<SkippableFact>]` mandate, now known to be unusable in this repo, and #17's
incorrect largest-diff positions), followed by the trivial **dead-reference
cleanup** (#14). Pass-1 gaps #3 and #7/#8 are minor: the first is a hedged
nice-to-have, and the latter two are under-specifications that the implementation
resolved correctly anyway by going back to the operator-supplied source the spec
points at.
